WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.580
Chile created tradable water rights to send every drop to its highest value use, and left some

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:13.300
rivers unable to reach the sea. This is Unintended Consequences, episode 133. In the dry valleys

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:18.280
of northern Chile, farmers once opened wooden gates at dawn to let water flow through

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:25.700
centuries-old Asakia channels that fed orchards and small plots. By the 2000s, those same channels

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:30.900
miles often stayed empty while large avocado orchards and copper mines upstream continued

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.140
pumping. The system had been built to move water efficiently to whoever valued it most.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:42.280
Instead, it severed the slow, repeated return flows that had kept entire downstream stretches

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:49.220
alive. In the 1980s, Chilean policymakers faced a familiar, erud zone problem. Water was

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:54.820
scarce in the north, yet existing rules gave little incentive for careful use or reallocation.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:01:00.800
They decided that clearly defined tradable property rights would let water move without

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.080
central command, toward mining, export agriculture, and cities that could pay more per cubic meter

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.540
than traditional smallholders.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.600
The approach drew on emerging ideas that secure ownership and voluntary exchange would

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.340
raise overall productivity while reducing waste.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:22.760
At the time, the northern regions were already under pressure from expanding copper operations

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:28.980
and new export crops, so a mechanism that rewarded conservation and efficient transfer looked

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.940
like a practical improvement over rigid administrative allocations. Officials expected that sellers

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:40.380
would only part with rights if they received fair compensation, and buyers would only purchase

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:45.060
what they truly needed. They reason that once rights carried a clear price signal,

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.500
users would compare the value of keeping water on their own land against the revenue from selling it.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:55.820
This logic assumed that the physical movement of water would remain compatible with the older

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.080
pattern of gradual downstream reuse.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:04.080
The 1980s reform established water rights as private property that could be bought, sold,

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:05.960
or leased separately from land.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.520
Early trades occurred mainly within river basins in the north.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.260
Proponents pointed to rising prices as evidence that water was reaching higher value uses.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:21.240
Mining companies and large fruit exporters became active buyers, while some smaller farmers

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.200
sold rights during dry years or when facing debt.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.460
Government records showed active markets forming in several basins.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:33.120
Initial studies suggested gains in economic output per unit of water.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.800
A few observers noted that the rules treated return flows as unowned.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:43.480
The dominant view held that market prices would internalize any important downstream effects.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:49.540
proponents argued that if downstream users truly depended on those flows, they would bid to keep

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:54.760
rights in the channel. Skeptics countered that the diffuse nature of seepage made such bidding

00:02:54.760 --> 00:03:00.020
impractical for dispersed smallholders who lacked the capital or organization to compete.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:06.260
Implementation proceeded basin by basin with rights recorded in registries that tracked ownership,

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:10.380
but did not require measurement of actual consumption versus return.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:16.780
In the first decade, trades concentrated in the Copiapo and Lemite systems, where mining

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.580
and table-grape expansion created steady demand.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.980
Once rights concentrated in the hands of a few large users, extraction patterns changed.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.660
Mines and avocado operations drew from upstream points and often returned little or nothing

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.120
to the channel, because their processes consumed the water or routed it elsewhere.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.140
However, traditional Assychia systems had relied on seapage and tail-end drainage that

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.140
recharged shallow aquifers and fed lower stretches.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.840
Those diffuse returns disappeared when water moved through sealed pipes or was fully consumed.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:54.800
Sections of rivers such as the Copiapo and parts of the Limati basin began to run dry

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:59.180
from months at a time, wetlands that depended on steady base flow shrank.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:04.300
Artisanal fishing communities along the coast noticed reduced freshwater pulses that had

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:10.660
supported estuarine species. Because the market only priced the water withdrawn, not the hydrologic

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:16.380
connectivity it once maintained, each rational trade improved the buyer's ledger while eroding

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:22.140
the unpriced services that downstream users had taken for granted. Over successive seasons,

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:27.780
the cumulative effect left some river mouths closed by sand bars for the first time in living memory.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.180
One might ask whether higher prices should have prompted buyers to lease rather than consume.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.180
tomb, yet the arithmetic favored full consumption.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:42.400
An avocado orchard or leaching operation could generate returns per cubic meter far above

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.820
the lease rate a downstream farmer could offer, so permanent transfer and total extraction

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.000
became the equilibrium.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.560
Downstream users could simply have purchased rights themselves in theory.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:59.200
The capital required to outbid a copper mine for an entire season's allocation exceeded

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:05.320
the annual revenue of most small Assychia associations. They remained unable to protect the return

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:10.780
flow loop on which their own plots depended. The result was not a single dramatic failure,

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:16.120
but a gradual unraveling in which each efficient transaction removed one more thread from the

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:22.380
fabric of shared recharge. By the 2010s, courts and basin authorities began receiving complaints

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:28.420
from downstream irrigators and environmental groups. New regulations introduced minimum ecological

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:34.380
flows and limited further trading in over-allocated basins, though existing rights remained largely

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:39.360
intact. Some communities experimented with collective repurchase of rights to restore

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:46.440
channel flow. The cost proved high once prices reflected mining and export demand. In a few basins,

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:52.400
the added restrictions themselves slowed needed transfers during extreme drought. Today, the core

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:57.600
market framework persists, but with tighter oversight and growing recognition that return flow

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.000
accounting must be built into the rights themselves.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:05.520
Pilot programs now require some new transfers to demonstrate that a portion of the water

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:06.800
will re-enter the channel.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.480
Enforcement depends on metering that remains uneven across remote valleys.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:16.540
The tension between preserving historic connectivity and allowing further reallocation

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:21.700
to high-value uses continues to shape every negotiation over additional rights.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:27.780
Markets for natural resources work best when the traded good is fully specified, including

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.380
the side effects that connect one user to another.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:36.040
Treating water as a simple commodity without modeling its passage through soil, aquifers,

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:40.460
and channels can erase the very connectivity that sustains the broader system.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:46.540
Before scaling any reallocation mechanism, it helps to map the invisible return paths

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:48.320
that existing users depend on.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:54.060
How might today's carbon or biodiversity credit markets avoid repeating the same oversight?

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:55.920
That's the case for today.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:00.500
The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:03.580
who'd find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening.

